{"agent":"main","body":{"claimed_done":true,"claimed_done_reason":"structural","final_message_bytes":1036,"final_message_hash":"sha256:b3fe0f3d62202ecb14f202d8c35e494351be7bcbacb02ae8af594a11fdae1ca9","final_message_inline":"Fixed in `inventory/reserve.py:19` — `Inventory` now holds a `threading.RLock` and each of `reserve`, `release`, and `available` does its store read-modify-write inside it. The store exposes no compare-and-set (and production swaps in a Redis-backed store with the same three methods), so the check-then-set in `reserve` had to be serialized at this layer; that's what let both SKU-4471 orders read 5 and both pass the stock check.\n\n- `inventory/store.py` and `tests/**` are untouched, per the contract's OUT list; the only edit is `inventory/reserve.py`.\n- Public signatures unchanged.\n- `python3 -m unittest tests.test_reserve`: 3 tests, OK (including the oversell reproduction).\n- `saga gate check`: G1, G2, G3 all MET. G3 reports \"UNPROVEN: declared none\" — that's the contract's own `RED: none`, not a failure.\n\nOne scope note: the lock is per-`Inventory` instance, so two `Inventory` objects sharing one store could still race. Making that safe needs an atomic operation on the store itself, which is out of bounds here.\n\nDONE","final_message_ref":null,"phase":"assistant_end"},"hash":"sha256:81ece796fee97c37657a4adb59a170947f119c0f851ca1d175a1ef1b6b77bec9","hook_ms":3,"masked_count":0,"mono_ns":48449051000,"prev":"sha256:ad26ff5717cf9ecebbe19cc5caa44ca71dd41b29f79a604738fc14dc5f363c34","schema":"saga.trace/1","seq":29,"session":"f59cde18-9ecd-4b21-946d-7b831968c38f","source":"hook:Stop","ts":"2026-09-13T04:45:35.343Z","turn":1,"type":"turn"}
